# =============================================================================
#  ec2-environment configuration
# =============================================================================
#
#      ec2 init_environment
#      $EDITOR ~/.config/ec2/environment
#
#  -- How to read this file ----------------------------------------------------
#
#  Almost every setting is commented out and shows the value the scripts fall
#  back to, so an untouched copy already behaves sensibly. Uncomment a line only
#  to change it; deleting a line is the same as leaving it commented.
#
#  The uncommented lines are the ones you have to look at:
#
#    * marked "# REQUIRED" and shipped empty - the scripts have no default and
#      scripts/variables.sh refuses to run until every one of them is set. It lists
#      all the missing ones at once, so you do not have to find them one by one.
#    * the naming helpers and the AMI/filter names built from them - they work
#      as shipped, but every AMI and `ec2` lookup is named after them, so set
#      RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX to something unique to you before the first build.
#
#  -- Who reads this file ------------------------------------------------------
#
#  ec2 environment commands source this file, apply their defaults and helpers,
#                       and finally verify that
#                       `aws sts get-caller-identity` works. If the credential
#                       provider needs an explicit refresh, AWS_AUTH_COMMAND is
#                       run; otherwise no provider-specific login is attempted.
#  ec2 make_ami         Writes $WORKDIR/packer/main.json and
#                       $WORKDIR/packer/variables_<family>.json, then runs
#                       `packer validate` and `packer build` for every enabled
#                       AMI family.
#  ec2 setup            Resolves (or creates) the shared file systems, writes
#                       one launch JSON per subnet and family under
#                       $WORKDIR/ec2/cli_input_json/, writes the `ec2` command
#                       configuration to $WORKDIR/ec2/config, and generates the
#                       cloud-init user-data at $WORKDIR/ec2/user_data.sh[.gz].
#
#      ec2 make_ami
#      ec2 setup
#
#  Both default to work-directory=~/.config/ec2/work and
#  environment-config=~/.config/ec2/environment. Override them with --workdir
#  and --environment-config when needed.
#
#  -- Syntax rules -------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  * This file is sourced by bash, so shell expansion works. $HOME, $PWD,
#    $WORKDIR and $CONFIG are already exported when it is sourced, and any
#    variable defined earlier in this file can be reused later on.
#  * Comma-separated lists ("a,b,c") must not contain spaces.
#  * Package lists are space separated, because they are handed to dnf/yum.
#  * Boolean values are not uniform: each one is forwarded verbatim to a
#    different consumer (Packer, run-instances JSON, the `ec2` command, shell
#    tests), so the accepted values are stated per entry. Do not guess.
#  * Anything named *_IDS can be given as *_NAMES instead, and bootstrap.sh looks
#    the IDs up for you. *_IDS wins when both are set. A name that matches
#    nothing, or more than one resource, is an error rather than a guess.
#  * Every *_IDS list must have as many entries as its *_MOUNT_POINTS list
#    (scripts/common.sh: compare_length).
#  * For the remaining per-file-system option lists, a single value is broadcast
#    to all entries; otherwise the length must match the number of names
#    (scripts/common.sh: make_array).
#
#  -- Per-AMI-family overrides -------------------------------------------------
#
#  Every variable consumed by the Packer template can be overridden for a single
#  AMI family by prefixing it with CPU_ or GPU_
#  (scripts/make_ami.sh: make_vars / family_value). For example:
#
#      AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_SIZE_GIB="60"        # applies to both families
#      GPU_AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_SIZE_GIB="200"   # GPU family only
#
#  Supported bases: OUTPUT_AMI_NAME, SOURCE_AMI_NAME_FILTER, SOURCE_AMI_OWNER,
#  SOURCE_AMI_ID, BUILD_INSTANCE_TYPE, AMI_BUILD_PROFILE, AMI_BUILD_REGION,
#  AMI_BUILD_SSH_INTERFACE, AMI_BUILD_SSH_USERNAME, AMI_BUILD_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS,
#  AMI_BUILD_SUBNET_ID, AMI_BUILD_VPC_ID, AMI_BUILD_IAM_INSTANCE_PROFILE,
#  AMI_ROOT_DEVICE_NAME, AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_DELETE_ON_TERMINATION, AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_SIZE_GIB,
#  AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_TYPE, AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_ENCRYPTED, AMI_ENABLE_SWAP, AMI_SWAP_BLOCK_SIZE, AMI_SWAP_BLOCK_COUNT, AMI_PACKAGES,
#  AMI_FLATPAK_PACKAGES, AMI_EXTRA_PACKAGES, AMI_TIMEZONE, AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_BACKEND,
#  AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_SCHEDULE, AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_KEEPALIVE_PROCESSES, AMI_ENABLE_SHARED_MEMORY,
#  AMI_UPDATE_PACKAGES.
#
#  -- Required settings --------------------------------------------------------
#
#  Needed by every command:
#
#      AWS_REGION              AWS region                             (section 2)
#
#  Needed by ec2 setup only - it places instances and describes how you
#  reach them, which building an AMI does not:
#
#      AWS_SUBNET_IDS          subnet(s) to deploy into               (section 3)
#                          (or AWS_SUBNET_NAMES)
#      EC2_KEY_NAME            EC2 key pair name                      (section 3)
#
#  The file system scripts also need AWS_SUBNET_IDS, but only to create something.
#
#  <FAMILY>_AMI_NAME is required as well for every family whose
#  <FAMILY>_INSTANCE is 1 - CPU by default, GPU once you enable it.
#
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
#  1. Naming helpers
# =============================================================================
#  Not read by any script. They only exist so that the AMI names, file system
#  names and `ec2` filters below can be derived from one place, which is why
#  they and the names built from them are left active. Rename or drop them if
#  you prefer to spell every name out.

# Bump this whenever AMI_PACKAGES, AMI_PROVISION_SCRIPTS or the source AMI filter changes, so that
# a new AMI name - and therefore a new AMI - is produced instead of colliding
# with the existing one. Packer fails on a duplicate AMI name.
AMI_REVISION=1

# Prefix for AMI names, file system names and the `ec2` command filters. Keep it
# unique per person when several people share one AWS account.
RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX="dev"


# =============================================================================
#  2. AWS account, region and API polling
# =============================================================================

# AWS CLI profile. Used for every aws call (--profile) and passed to Packer as
# `profile`.
# AWS CLI credentials are used locally and are not automatically written into
# user-data. Explicit settings such as INSTANCE_COPY_ENTRIES and
# INSTANCE_USER_DATA_EXTRA_SCRIPT can still embed sensitive contents; review
# the generated user-data before launching.
# Default: default
#AWS_PROFILE="default"

# Optional command used to refresh credentials after the AWS CLI credential
# provider chain reports an authentication failure. This is not limited to SSO:
# it can call `aws sso login`, aws-vault, a credential broker, or another local
# authentication helper. Use single quotes when the command refers to variables.
#AWS_AUTH_COMMAND='aws sso login --profile "$AWS_PROFILE"'

# AWS region. Required - scripts/variables.sh aborts when it is empty. Used for
# every aws call (--region), for Packer's `region`, and inside user-data to
# build the FSx NFS endpoint <fs-id>.fsx.<region>.amazonaws.com.
AWS_REGION=""   # REQUIRED

# Exported by scripts/make_ami.sh as AWS_POLL_DELAY_SECONDS / AWS_MAX_ATTEMPTS, and
# picked up by the AWS SDK that Packer uses. They bound how long Packer waits
# for AWS state changes such as "AMI available". The defaults allow roughly
# 10 s x 400 = 66 minutes, which is enough for a large image with many packages.
# Raise AWS_MAX_ATTEMPTS if a build times out while snapshotting.
#AWS_POLL_DELAY_SECONDS=10
#AWS_MAX_ATTEMPTS=400

# Packer action for resources it created when a build fails:
#   cleanup  Remove temporary build resources (recommended).
#   abort    Leave resources for manual inspection.
#   ask      Ask interactively (not suitable for unattended builds).
# Default: cleanup
#AMI_PACKER_ON_ERROR="cleanup"

# Optional external Packer JSON template. When set, this replaces the generated
# default template; it must use the variables written to variables_<family>.json.
#AMI_PACKER_TEMPLATE_FILE=""

# Behavior when an AMI with the configured output name already exists:
#   build   Start a new build (default, preserving existing behavior).
#   reuse   Skip Packer and use the newest self-owned AMI with that name.
#   fail    Abort before starting any AMI build.
# Default: build
#AMI_EXISTING_IMAGE_ACTION="build"

# Interval in seconds for the progress display while Packer is running.
# This controls only status polling and does not change Packer's AWS SDK polling.
# Default: 15
#AMI_PROGRESS_POLL_DELAY_SECONDS=15
#AMI_PROGRESS_MAX_ATTEMPTS=400

# Number of attempts and delay (seconds) used by instance user-data while
# waiting for requested mounts and attached block devices. Provisioning fails
# without creating ~/ready when a required mount never becomes usable.
#MOUNT_READY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=60
#MOUNT_READY_RETRY_INTERVAL_SECONDS=2


# =============================================================================
#  3. Network and IAM
# =============================================================================

# VPC used by the Packer build instance (Packer `vpc_id`). Optional: Packer
# resolves it from the subnet when it is empty, and the only thing it needs the
# VPC for is creating a temporary security group, which it never does here
# because AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS is always passed.
#
# Set it to have Packer fail early on a mismatch: it must be the VPC that owns
# AWS_SUBNET_IDS and AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS.
# Default: empty, resolved from AMI_BUILD_SUBNET_ID
#AWS_VPC_ID=""

# Name tag of the VPC, looked up when AWS_VPC_ID is empty.
#AWS_VPC_NAME=""

# Subnets to deploy into, comma separated. Required by scripts/setup_ec2.sh, and by
# the file system scripts when they create something. make_ami works without it:
# Packer then picks a subnet in the default VPC. More than one may be
# listed: scripts/setup_ec2.sh then writes one launch JSON per subnet per enabled
# AMI family, so an instance can be started in whichever availability zone has
# capacity, and scripts/setup_efs.sh adds a mount target in every subnet listed
# here.
#
# Two of the file system kinds restrict how many entries are allowed, and both
# checks run even when that kind is unused, because scripts/setup_ec2.sh invokes
# every setup script unconditionally:
#   * scripts/setup_io2.sh aborts on more than one subnet - an io2 volume has to be
#     created in the availability zone the instance runs in.
#   * scripts/setup_fsx.sh aborts when FSX_DEPLOYMENT_TYPE is SINGLE_AZ_* and more
#     than one subnet is given, and when it is MULTI_AZ_* and only one is.
AWS_SUBNET_IDS=""   # REQUIRED by setup_ec2

# Name tags of the subnets, comma separated, looked up when AWS_SUBNET_IDS is empty.
# The resolved order follows the order written here, so EC2_SUBNET_LABELS still
# lines up.
#AWS_SUBNET_NAMES=""

# Short label per subnet, comma separated, in the same order as AWS_SUBNET_IDS.
# Only used to name the generated launch JSON files:
#     $WORKDIR/ec2/cli_input_json/<AMI_NAME>-<label>.json
# When a label is missing, scripts/setup_ec2.sh falls back to the subnet ID with
# the "subnet-" prefix stripped, which makes for long file names. Using the
# availability-zone letter ("a", "c", "d") keeps them readable.
# Default: empty, i.e. every file name carries the subnet ID.
#EC2_SUBNET_LABELS="a,d"

# Subnet used by the Packer build instance (Packer `subnet_id`).
# Leave this commented out to use the first entry of AWS_SUBNET_IDS, which is the
# normal case. Uncomment it only when the AMI must be built in a different
# subnet from the one the instances are launched in. An empty value is *not* the
# same as leaving it out: scripts/bootstrap.sh only fills in the default when the
# variable is undefined, so AMI_BUILD_SUBNET_ID="" means "let Packer choose a subnet".
#AMI_BUILD_SUBNET_ID=""

# Security groups, comma separated. Optional, but you almost certainly want to
# set it. They are applied to both
#   * the Packer build instance (Packer `security_group_ids`), and
#   * the network interface in the generated launch JSON ("Groups").
# They must therefore allow whatever AMI_BUILD_SSH_INTERFACE needs (inbound 22 from the
# host that runs Packer) plus the NFS/EFS/mosh traffic you expect at run time.
#
# Leaving it empty is a real downgrade, not just a default:
#   * Packer builds a throwaway security group for the build instance and
#     authorises it from `temporary_security_group_source_cidrs`, which defaults
#     to 0.0.0.0/0. Inbound SSH is open to the internet for the whole build.
#   * The launch JSON leaves "Groups" out, so instances get the VPC default
#     security group - whatever that happens to allow.
# Default: empty
#AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS=""

# Security group names, comma separated, looked up when AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS is
# empty. These are real group names (the EC2 GroupName attribute), not Name
# tags. Group names are unique within a VPC, so set AWS_VPC_ID as well when the same
# name exists in more than one VPC.
#AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_NAMES=""

# Name of the IAM instance profile, used both for the launched instances
# ("IamInstanceProfile" in the generated launch JSON) and for the Packer build
# instance (Packer `iam_instance_profile`). An ARN is not accepted here.
#
# Leave it empty to run without an instance profile - but the instance then
# cannot use the AWS credential chain, cannot attach io2 volumes from user-data,
# and cannot reach S3/EFS/FSx with IAM authentication.
#
# The role needs at least: ec2:DescribeInstances, ec2:AttachVolume (io2),
# elasticfilesystem client mount permissions (EFS), and whatever your own
# INSTANCE_USER_DATA_EXTRA_SCRIPT hook does.
#AWS_IAM_INSTANCE_PROFILE=""

# EC2 key pair name written to "KeyName" in the generated launch JSON. Required
# for SSH access to the launched instances. Packer does not use it: it creates
# and discards a temporary key pair of its own for the build.
EC2_KEY_NAME=""   # REQUIRED by setup_ec2


# =============================================================================
#  4. AMI build - build instance access
# =============================================================================

# Default login user of the source AMI. Not used directly; it seeds the
# defaults of SSH_USERNAME and EC2_SSH_USERNAME. "ec2-user" for Amazon Linux and the
# Deep Learning AMIs, "ubuntu" for Ubuntu images.
# Default: ec2-user
#EC2_USER="ec2-user"

# How Packer connects to the build instance (Packer `ssh_interface`):
#   private_ip       Connect to the private IP. Requires that the host running
#                    Packer can reach the VPC (VPN, Direct Connect, same VPC).
#   public_ip        Connect to the public IP. Needs a public subnet and
#                    auto-assigned public IP.
#   private_dns / public_dns
#   session_manager  Connect through SSM, no inbound rule needed. Requires an
#                    SSM-capable AWS_IAM_INSTANCE_PROFILE on the build instance.
# Default: private_ip
#AMI_BUILD_SSH_INTERFACE="private_ip"

# SSH user Packer logs in as (Packer `ssh_username`), also exported to the
# provisioning scripts as $SSH_USERNAME.
# Default: value of EC2_USER
#AMI_BUILD_SSH_USERNAME="${EC2_USER}"


# =============================================================================
#  5. AMI build - root volume
# =============================================================================
#  These become Packer's launch_block_device_mappings, i.e. the root volume of
#  the build instance and therefore of the resulting AMI. The generated launch
#  JSON does not override block devices, so every instance started from the AMI
#  inherits exactly this root volume.

# Root device name of the source AMI. /dev/xvda for Amazon Linux 2023,
# /dev/sda1 for most Ubuntu images. A wrong value creates a second, unused
# volume instead of resizing the root one.
# Default: /dev/xvda
#AMI_ROOT_DEVICE_NAME="/dev/xvda"

# Root volume size in GiB. Must be at least the size of the source AMI's
# snapshot. The Deep Learning AMIs need considerably more than Amazon Linux, so
# GPU_VOLUME_SIZE is a common override.
# Default: 60
#AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_SIZE_GIB="60"

# Root volume type: gp3, gp2, io1, io2, standard.
# Default: gp3
#AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_TYPE="gp3"

# Encrypt the root volume with the account's default EBS key. "true" or "false".
# Default: true
#AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_ENCRYPTED="true"

# Delete the root volume when the instance terminates. "true" or "false".
# Set true only for disposable instances. When false, the root volume remains
# after termination and must be deleted separately if it is no longer needed.
# Default: false
#AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_DELETE_ON_TERMINATION="false"


# =============================================================================
#  6. AMI build - provisioning
# =============================================================================
#  ec2 make_ami extracts its built-in Packer assets to $WORKDIR/packer/ and runs
#  Packer from there, so additional AMI_PROVISION_SCRIPTS paths are relative to that directory. Every
#  script runs as root ("sudo -S -E sh -eux") and receives the settings below as
#  environment variables.

# Optional comma-separated user scripts appended after enabled built-in features.
# Paths are relative to the Packer working directory.
#AMI_PROVISION_SCRIPTS=""

# Swap file at /swapfile, created with `dd bs=$AMI_SWAP_BLOCK_SIZE count=$AMI_SWAP_BLOCK_COUNT`,
# activated and appended to /etc/fstab. 128M x 64 = 8 GiB. Set either one to an
# empty string to skip swap entirely. Skipped when /swapfile already exists.
# Set AMI_ENABLE_SWAP=1 to enable this feature; it defaults to 0.
# Remember that the swap file consumes AMI_ROOT_VOLUME_SIZE_GIB.
# Defaults: 128M, 64
#AMI_ENABLE_SWAP=0
#AMI_SWAP_BLOCK_SIZE="128M"
#AMI_SWAP_BLOCK_COUNT="64"

# 1 ensures `/dev/shm` has mode 1777, so that unprivileged multi-process
# workloads (PyTorch DataLoader workers, joblib, multiprocessing) can use it.
# Any other value skips it.
#AMI_ENABLE_SHARED_MEMORY=0

# System time zone, applied with `timedatectl set-timezone`. Any tz database
# name, e.g. UTC, Asia/Tokyo, America/Los_Angeles. A non-empty value
# automatically enables the step; empty (the default) skips it.
#AMI_TIMEZONE=""

# Space-separated packages installed with the selected package manager. Applies to
# both AMI families; use CPU_AMI_EXTRA_PACKAGES / GPU_AMI_EXTRA_PACKAGES for
# family-specific extras.
#
# packer/scripts/install_packages.sh special-cases a few names:
#   gh                            Adds the cli.github.com yum repository first.
#   terraform                     Adds the HashiCorp repository first.
#   lazygit                       Enables copr dejan/lazygit
#                                 (amazonlinux-2023-x86_64 only).
#   google-chrome                 Downloaded and installed from the direct rpm.
#   spal, spal-release            Installs the spal-release repository package.
#
# Add "amazon-efs-utils" when you mount EFS, and "docker" when using Docker.
# dnf/yum support the repository-backed special packages above; apt supports
# ordinary packages and Flatpak. The script exits for unsupported special names.
#AMI_PACKAGES=""

# Package manager backend. `auto` detects dnf, yum, or apt-get in that order.
# Set this explicitly when an image provides more than one backend.
# Default: auto
#AMI_PACKAGE_MANAGER="auto"

# 1 applies the package manager's update before package installation; 0 installs against the
# package versions exposed by the selected source AMI and its configured repos.
# The final NEVRA list is always recorded in
# /var/log/ec2-environment-packages.tsv inside the built AMI.
# The package setup runs automatically when AMI_PACKAGES or
# AMI_FLATPAK_PACKAGES is non-empty, or when AMI_UPDATE_PACKAGES=1.
# Default: 0. Set to 1 to update packages even when no package is installed.
#AMI_UPDATE_PACKAGES=0

# Space-separated Flatpak application IDs. When non-empty, flatpak is installed,
# the flathub remote is added, and these are installed from it. Leave empty to
# skip Flatpak completely.
#AMI_FLATPAK_PACKAGES=""

# Idle auto-shutdown, installed as /usr/local/sbin/shutdown_if_idle.
# Set AMI_ENABLE_IDLE_SHUTDOWN=1 to enable this feature; it defaults to 0.
#   systemd   Install shutdown.service plus shutdown.timer and enable them.
#   cron      Drop a script into /etc/cron.<AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_SCHEDULE>/shutdown.
#   ""        Skip the whole step; the instance never powers itself off.
# Any other value writes the helper script but schedules nothing, which is the
# same as "" in practice.
# Default backend: systemd
#AMI_ENABLE_IDLE_SHUTDOWN=0
#AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_BACKEND="systemd"

# How often idleness is checked. The accepted values depend on AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_BACKEND:
#   systemd   A systemd OnCalendar= expression - hourly, daily,
#             "*:0/30" (every 30 minutes), and so on.
#   cron      A cron directory suffix - hourly, daily, weekly or monthly.
#             /etc/cron.<value>/ has to exist on the image.
# The check powers off only if the instance still looks idle after a further
# 60 s, so the worst case wasted is one interval.
# Default: hourly
#AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_SCHEDULE="hourly"

# Extended-regular-expression alternation of process names that keep the
# instance alive, so that a detached session is not powered off. Matched against
# `ps -eo comm=` for processes owned by a non-root user, anchored at both ends,
# and baked into shutdown_if_idle when the AMI is built.
#
# This matters because `who` does not list detached screen/tmux sessions, mosh
# sessions, or `ssh host command` without a tty. Leave it empty to power off
# purely on `who`. Note that Linux truncates `comm` to 15 characters, so match
# on the short process name.
#AMI_IDLE_SHUTDOWN_KEEPALIVE_PROCESSES="sshd|screen|tmux|mosh-server"


# =============================================================================
#  7. AMI families
# =============================================================================
#  Families listed here are built by scripts/make_ami.sh only when
#  <FAMILY>_ENABLED=1, and get their own launch JSON
#  from scripts/setup_ec2.sh. Everything in sections 4 to 6 can be overridden per
#  family with a <FAMILY>_ prefix.

# Comma-separated family names. CPU and GPU are the built-in examples; additional
# families require matching <FAMILY>_ settings below or in a custom environment.
# Default: CPU,GPU
#AMI_FAMILIES="CPU,GPU"

# --- CPU family --------------------------------------------------------------

# 1 builds this AMI and generates its launch JSONs; 0 skips the family entirely.
# Default: 1
#CPU_ENABLED=1

# Required while CPU_ENABLED is 1.
# Name of the AMI to build, and the name scripts/setup_ec2.sh looks up with
# `describe-images --owners self` to find the image ID (the newest match by
# creation date wins, and the result is cached in $WORKDIR/ami_ids).
# Because Packer refuses to overwrite an existing AMI name, include AMI_REVISION and
# bump it for every rebuild.
CPU_OUTPUT_AMI_NAME="${RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX}-cpu-${AMI_REVISION}"

# Source AMI name filter. Wildcards are allowed. The generator resolves the
# newest match to one exact AMI ID before invoking Packer and records that ID in
# build-inputs-cpu.json.
#CPU_SOURCE_AMI_NAME_FILTER="al2023-ami-*-x86_64" # Amazon Linux 2023

# Owner of the source AMI: "amazon", "self", or an account ID.
#CPU_SOURCE_AMI_OWNER="amazon"

# Optional exact source AMI. When set, this takes precedence over the filter and
# makes repeated builds independent of newly published upstream images.
#CPU_SOURCE_AMI_ID=""

# Instance type used to build the AMI, and the InstanceType written into this
# family's launch JSON. It must match the architecture of CPU_SOURCE_AMI_NAME_FILTER
# (x86_64 vs arm64). A larger type shortens the build but costs more per hour.
#CPU_BUILD_INSTANCE_TYPE="c8i.2xlarge"

# Space-separated packages appended to AMI_PACKAGES for this family only.
#CPU_AMI_EXTRA_PACKAGES=""

# --- GPU family --------------------------------------------------------------

# 1 builds this AMI and generates its launch JSONs; 0 skips the family entirely.
# Default: 1
#GPU_ENABLED=0

# Required once GPU_ENABLED is 1 - uncomment it together with GPU_ENABLED.
#GPU_OUTPUT_AMI_NAME="${RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX}-gpu-${AMI_REVISION}"

# The Deep Learning AMI names end with a release date, hence the trailing
# wildcard. The newest match is resolved once before Packer starts.
#GPU_SOURCE_AMI_NAME_FILTER="Deep Learning OSS Nvidia Driver AMI GPU PyTorch * (Amazon Linux 2023)*"

#GPU_SOURCE_AMI_OWNER="amazon"

#GPU_SOURCE_AMI_ID=""

# Must be a GPU type, otherwise the NVIDIA driver in the source AMI has no
# device to bind to.
#GPU_BUILD_INSTANCE_TYPE="g4dn.xlarge"

# Space-separated packages appended to AMI_PACKAGES for this family only. Useful for
# resolving conflicts with what the Deep Learning AMI already ships, for example
# putting openssl-devel in CPU_AMI_EXTRA_PACKAGES because the GPU image already
# has openssl11-devel.
#GPU_AMI_EXTRA_PACKAGES=""


# =============================================================================
#  8. Shared file systems
# =============================================================================
#  Four kinds of storage can be attached, and each one follows the same pattern:
#
#    <KIND>_IDS           Existing resource IDs, comma separated. Used as is.
#    <KIND>_NAMES         Names to look up when <KIND>_IDS is empty. Resolved
#                         IDs replace it.
#    <KIND>_MOUNT_POINTS  Mount point per resolved ID, in the same order. The
#                         number of entries must match IDS, or NAMES when IDs
#                         are resolved.
#
#  Leave both IDS and NAMES empty to skip a kind completely. Each name is
#  resolved independently, preserving input order. Missing EFS / FSx / io2
#  names are created from the remaining settings if CREATE_FILE_SYSTEMS=1; if
#  it is 0, all missing names are reported before the run aborts. Resolution and
#  creation happen in scripts/setup_ec2.sh, which calls scripts/setup_s3files.sh,
#  setup_efs, setup_fsx and setup_io2 in that order.
#
#  The resulting mounts are written into the generated user-data as /etc/fstab
#  entries followed by `mount -a`, all with `nofail`, so a broken mount does not
#  prevent the instance from booting - check `mount` after login.

# 1 allows scripts/setup_ec2.sh to create missing EFS / FSx / io2 resources.
# 0 (recommended for day-to-day use) makes it fail instead, so a typo in a name
# cannot silently bill you for a new file system. Creation is a one-off step:
# set it to 1 once, then put it back to 0.
# Default: 0
#CREATE_FILE_SYSTEMS=0

# Comma-separated filesystem providers used by `ec2 setup`. Each provider maps
# to scripts/setup_<provider>.sh and returns comma-separated IDs on stdout; this
# makes custom providers addable without editing setup_ec2.sh. A provider can
# additionally write $WORKDIR/ec2/mounts/<provider>.sh to append its mount setup
# to generated user-data. The built-in providers are s3files, efs, fsx, and io2.
# Removing one skips its lookup, creation, and mount entries.
# Default: s3files,efs,fsx,io2
#EC2_FILESYSTEM_PROVIDERS="s3files,efs,fsx,io2"

# Optional directory containing executable <provider>.sh cleanup hooks. A hook
# receives the resource ID and name, and must verify ownership before deleting
# or accepting an already-absent resource. Non-zero status keeps the manifest row.
#EC2_FILESYSTEM_CLEANUP_HOOK_DIR=""

# Print the missing-resource creation plan without creating anything. This is
# useful with CREATE_FILE_SYSTEMS=1 before an apply run; generated launch files
# omit IDs for resources that only appeared in the dry-run plan.
#RESOURCE_DRY_RUN=0

# Every created AMI, EFS, FSx, and io2 resource receives ManagedBy plus the
# non-empty metadata tags below. Exact created IDs are appended to the manifest,
# which scripts/cleanup_resources.sh consumes in dry-run mode by default.
#RESOURCE_MANAGED_BY="ec2-environment"
#RESOURCE_PROJECT=""
#RESOURCE_OWNER=""
# ISO-8601 is recommended, for example 2026-12-31T00:00:00Z.
#RESOURCE_EXPIRES_AT=""
#RESOURCE_MANIFEST="my_work/resources.tsv"

# --- S3-backed file systems (aws s3files) ------------------------------------
#  Mounted as: <id>:/  on  <mount point>  type s3files  (_netdev,nofail)
#  These are never created by this toolkit: names are looked up with
#  `aws s3files list-file-systems`, and the run fails when a name cannot be
#  resolved. Requires an AWS CLI that provides the `s3files` command and an
#  instance able to authenticate to it.
#S3FILES_IDS=""
#S3FILES_NAMES=""
#S3FILES_MOUNT_POINTS="/mnt/s3files"

# --- EFS ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Mounted as: <id>:/  on  <mount point>  type efs
#              (_netdev,tls,noresvport,nofail)
#  The efs type and the tls option come from amazon-efs-utils, so keep
#  "amazon-efs-utils" in AMI_PACKAGES.
#EFS_IDS=""

# Looked up by the Name tag. When creation is enabled, a new file system is
# created with --backup --encrypted, tagged with this name, and a mount target
# is added in every subnet of AWS_SUBNET_IDS.
#EFS_NAMES=""

#EFS_MOUNT_POINTS="/mnt/efs"

# Security groups for the EFS mount targets, one per name (a single value is
# applied to all). Required only when EFS is created here. It must allow inbound
# NFS (2049) from AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS.
#EFS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS=""

# Group names, looked up when EFS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS is empty.
#EFS_SECURITY_GROUP_NAMES=""

# One per name, or a single value for all.
#   generalPurpose  Lowest latency. The right choice for interactive work.
#   maxIO           Higher throughput, higher latency, for massively parallel
#                   access.
# Default: generalPurpose
#EFS_PERFORMANCE_MODES="generalPurpose"

# One per name, or a single value for all: bursting, elastic, provisioned.
# "elastic" costs more per GB but removes the burst-credit cliff.
# Default: bursting
#EFS_THROUGHPUT_MODES="bursting"

# --- FSx for OpenZFS ---------------------------------------------------------
#  Mounted as: <id>.fsx.<AWS_REGION>.amazonaws.com:/fsx/  on  <mount point>
#              type nfs4 (noatime,nfsvers=4.2,nconnect=16,_netdev,nofail)
#  Much faster than EFS for many small files, which makes it a good home for
#  USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR below.
#FSX_IDS=""

# Looked up by the Name tag.
#FSX_NAMES=""

#FSX_MOUNT_POINTS="/mnt/fsx"

# Security groups for the file system, one per name or a single value for all.
# Required only when FSx is created here. Must allow inbound NFS (2049) plus the
# OpenZFS RPC ports from AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS.
#FSX_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS=""

# Group names, looked up when FSX_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS is empty.
#FSX_SECURITY_GROUP_NAMES=""

# SINGLE_AZ_1, SINGLE_AZ_2, MULTI_AZ_1. SINGLE_AZ_* requires exactly one entry
# in AWS_SUBNET_IDS; MULTI_AZ_* requires at least two. The first subnet becomes
# PreferredSubnetId.
# Default: SINGLE_AZ_1
#FSX_DEPLOYMENT_TYPE="SINGLE_AZ_1"

# SSD or INTELLIGENT_TIERING. FSX_STORAGE_CAPACITIES_GIB is only sent for SSD;
# INTELLIGENT_TIERING sizes itself.
# Default: SSD
#FSX_STORAGE_TYPES="SSD"

# Size in GiB per name, or a single value for all. SSD accepts 64 to 524288.
# Default: 100
#FSX_STORAGE_CAPACITIES_GIB="100"

# Throughput in MB/s per name, or a single value for all. Must be an integer
# from the values the deployment type allows (64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, ...).
# Default: 160
#FSX_THROUGHPUT_CAPACITIES_MIBPS="160"

# Daily automatic backup retention in days, per name or a single value for all.
# Must be an integer; 0 disables automatic backups.
# Default: 30
#FSX_AUTOMATIC_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS="30"

# Route tables that get the FSx routes, per name or a single value for all.
# Only used for MULTI_AZ_* deployments, and ignored otherwise.
#FSX_ROUTE_TABLE_IDS=""

# Name tags of the route tables, looked up when FSX_ROUTE_TABLE_IDS is empty.
#FSX_ROUTE_TABLE_NAMES=""

# --- io2 EBS volumes ---------------------------------------------------------
#  Not a shared file system: one volume is attached to one instance at a time.
#  At boot, user-data runs `aws ec2 attach-volume` - so the instance profile
#  needs ec2:AttachVolume - then waits up to 60 s for the device to appear by
#  matching the volume ID against the disk serial, runs mkfs when the disk has
#  no file system yet, mounts it, and adds a UUID-based /etc/fstab entry.
#
#  Requires exactly one entry in AWS_SUBNET_IDS, because the volume must be created
#  in the same availability zone as the instance. scripts/setup_io2.sh enforces
#  this even when io2 is unused.
#IO2_IDS=""

# Name tag for volumes to create. Leave empty unless you are creating volumes.
#IO2_NAMES=""

#IO2_MOUNT_POINTS="/mnt/io2"

# Size in GiB per name, or a single value for all.
# Default: 100
#IO2_VOLUME_SIZES_GIB="100"

# Provisioned IOPS per name, or a single value for all. io2 allows 100 to 64000,
# capped at 500 IOPS per GiB.
# Default: 3000
#IO2_VOLUME_IOPS="3000"

# Requested device name per volume, or a single value for all. Nitro instances
# rename it to /dev/nvme*, which is why the mount step resolves the real path
# from the volume serial instead of trusting this value.
# Default: /dev/sdf
#IO2_DEVICE_NAMES="/dev/sdf"

# File system created on a blank volume, per volume or a single value for all:
# xfs, ext4. Existing file systems are detected with blkid and left alone.
# Default: xfs
#IO2_FSTYPES="xfs"


# =============================================================================
#  9. Launch JSON and the `ec2` command
# =============================================================================
#  scripts/setup_ec2.sh writes one --cli-input-json file per subnet and enabled
#  family to $WORKDIR/ec2/cli_input_json/<AMI_NAME>-<label>.json, holding
#  ImageId, InstanceType, KeyName, EbsOptimized, IamInstanceProfile and a single
#  network interface. Everything else below is copied into $WORKDIR/ec2/config,
#  which the external `ec2` command reads. That config (minus ssh_key) and the
#  launch JSONs are installed on the instance under ~/.config/ec2/ as well, so
#  an instance can launch further instances.

# Substring the `ec2` command uses to find *your* instances by Name tag. Keep it
# narrow enough that you never operate on someone else's instance.
EC2_NAME_FILTER="${RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX}"

# Substring the `ec2` command uses to list *your* AMIs.
EC2_IMAGE_NAME_FILTER="${RESOURCE_NAME_PREFIX}"

# Optional local path to the private key matching EC2_KEY_NAME, typically
# ~/.ssh/<EC2_KEY_NAME>.pem. When empty, ssh/scp/mosh/et use the usual OpenSSH
# identity selection, including ssh-agent and ~/.ssh/config. The ec2 command
# normally connects by IP address, so any Host rule must match that address or
# pattern.
# This value is written to $WORKDIR/ec2/config only, never into user-data or the
# config installed on the instance.
#EC2_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY=""

# Additional SSH arguments copied to the generated ec2 config. Each array
# element is preserved as one argument for ssh/scp and rsync's SSH transport,
# and converted to Eternal Terminal's --ssh-option form for `ec2 et`. ET
# forwarding supports -o VALUE and -i KEY; use EC2_ET_OPTIONS or ~/.ssh/config
# for other ET-specific needs.
#EC2_SSH_OPTIONS=()

# Native Eternal Terminal client arguments, for example (--keepalive 30) or
# (--port 2022). Do not include the destination host.
#EC2_ET_OPTIONS=()

# Native scp arguments. Recursive copies are enabled by default; use () to
# disable that default, or add -p to preserve modification times. SSH connection
# arguments still belong in EC2_SSH_OPTIONS.
#EC2_SCP_OPTIONS=(-r)

# Native rsync arguments. The default archive mode supports recursive directory
# synchronization while preserving metadata. Do not set -e/--rsh here; ec2
# constructs it from EC2_SSH_OPTIONS and EC2_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY.
#EC2_RSYNC_OPTIONS=(-a)

# Login user on the launched instances. Also the user whose home directory the
# generated user-data sets up: dotfiles, ~/.aws, ~/.ssh, ~/.config/ec2,
# ~/ready.
# Default: value of EC2_USER
#EC2_SSH_USERNAME="${EC2_USER}"

# Connection backend used by interactive `ec2 ssh`. `ssh` uses the configured
# IP address and OpenSSH; `ssm` uses AWS Systems Manager Session Manager and
# requires the instance to be managed by SSM with the required IAM permissions.
# SSM currently supports interactive `ec2 ssh` only. File transfers, commands,
# mosh, and Eternal Terminal continue to use SSH.
# Default: ssh
#EC2_CONNECTION_METHOD="ssh"
# Set `ec2_instance_connect` to send the configured private key's public key
# through EC2 Instance Connect before opening an interactive SSH session.

# "AssociatePublicIpAddress" in the launch JSON. Must be the literal true or
# false - scripts/setup_ec2.sh rejects anything else. Set true only for public
# subnets; combine with EC2_USE_PRIVATE_IP=0 to actually connect over it.
#EC2_ASSOCIATE_PUBLIC_IP=false

# Tells the `ec2` command to connect to the private address. 1 or 0. Note the
# different value style from EC2_ASSOCIATE_PUBLIC_IP: this one is a flag for the `ec2`
# command, not a field in the launch JSON.
# Default: 1
#EC2_USE_PRIVATE_IP=1

# Default instance type for the `ec2` command when it is not taking the type
# from a launch JSON. Handy for a small "just give me a shell" instance that is
# cheaper than CPU_BUILD_INSTANCE_TYPE.
#EC2_DEFAULT_INSTANCE_TYPE="t3.medium"

# Request spot instances. 1 or 0. Much cheaper, but the instance can be
# reclaimed at any time, so pair it with the retry settings below and keep your
# state on the shared file systems.
# Default: 0
#EC2_USE_SPOT_INSTANCE=0

# Enable the `ec2` command's submit mode, which launches an instance, runs a
# command over SSH and shuts down afterwards. 1 or 0.
# Default: 1
#EC2_SUBMIT_COMMAND=1

# Submit-mode retries. The launch pair covers "no capacity" and spot rejections,
# the ssh pair covers the window between "running" and "sshd is accepting
# connections". Intervals are in seconds, so the defaults wait up to
# 120 x 60 s = 2 hours for capacity and 10 x 10 s = 100 s for SSH.
#EC2_SUBMIT_N_RETRY_LAUNCH=120
#EC2_SUBMIT_RETRY_LAUNCH_INTERVAL=60
#EC2_SUBMIT_N_RETRY_SSH=10
#EC2_SUBMIT_RETRY_SSH_INTERVAL=10

# Which generated group of launch JSONs the `ec2` command uses by default:
# "cpu" or "gpu". The groups themselves are filled in automatically from the
# enabled families.
# Default: cpu
#EC2_CLI_INPUT_JSON_GROUP="cpu"

# Path to mosh-server on the instance, passed to the `ec2` command. Point it at
# the distribution package (/usr/bin/mosh-server, needs "mosh" in AMI_PACKAGES) or
# at a copy on a shared file system, for example
# "${USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR}/usr/bin/mosh-server" when you install mosh through USER_ENV_INSTALLER_SCRIPTS.
# Default: /usr/bin/mosh-server
#EC2_MOSH_SERVER_PATH="/usr/bin/mosh-server"

# URI of the generated cloud-init user-data, as the `ec2` command expects it.
# scripts/setup_ec2.sh also derives the local output path from this value: it
# strips the scheme, and a ".gz" suffix additionally triggers gzip of the
# generated script. Compression can make a larger script fit in the 16 KB
# limit, but it does not encrypt the contents.
# Use "fileb://" for the compressed form and "file://" for plain text.
# User-data encoding. `auto` preserves the current behavior: a .gz URI creates
# gzip data and any other URI creates plain text. Set plain or gzip explicitly
# when the URI name should not determine the payload format.
# Default: auto
#EC2_USER_DATA_FORMAT="auto"

# Maximum user-data payload size accepted before launch. AWS EC2 currently
# limits the payload to 16384 bytes before API Base64 encoding. With gzip this
# is the compressed size; with plain it is the uncompressed script size. Lower
# it when a downstream launcher has a smaller limit.
# Default: 16384
#EC2_USER_DATA_MAX_BYTES=16384

# cloud-init and completion-marker paths used on the launched instance.
#EC2_CLOUD_INIT_INSTANCE_DIR="/var/lib/cloud/instances"
#EC2_READY_FILENAME="ready"
# Default: fileb://$WORKDIR/ec2/user_data.sh.gz (made absolute automatically)
#EC2_USER_DATA_URI="fileb://${WORKDIR}/ec2/user_data.sh.gz"


# =============================================================================
#  10. On-instance setup (generated user-data)
# =============================================================================
#  Everything below is rendered into $WORKDIR/ec2/user_data.sh and runs as root
#  through cloud-init on first boot, in this order: mounts (section 8), systemd services,
#  USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR, USER_ENV_INSTALLER_SCRIPTS, dotfiles, ~/.config/ec2, ~/.aws, ~/.ssh, and finally
#  your own INSTANCE_USER_DATA_EXTRA_SCRIPT. The script ends by copying
#  /var/log/cloud-init-output.log to ~/ready, which is the marker to poll for
#  when you want to know that setup has finished.
#
#  SECURITY: user-data is readable from inside the instance through the
#  instance metadata service, and a copy is left in the home directory. Do not
#  put access keys, API keys or private keys in user-data unless this exposure
#  is explicitly acceptable. Prefer AWS_IAM_INSTANCE_PROFILE and the AWS
#  credential chain for AWS access.

# Comma-separated systemd services to enable and start on first boot. Service
# names may contain letters, numbers, `_`, `.`, `@`, `:`, and `-`.
# Default: empty
#INSTANCE_SYSTEMD_SERVICES=""


# Directory on a shared file system that holds the things you want to survive
# instance termination: locally built tools and your dotfiles. Normally one of
# the mount points from section 8, for example "/mnt/fsx/fs2".
#
# Leave empty to disable USER_ENV_ENABLE_USR_SYMLINK, USER_ENV_INSTALLER_SCRIPTS and all USER_ENV_DOTFILES_* handling -
# they all live under this directory. setup_ec2 warns about the enabled settings
# it ignores, then continues generating the remaining configuration.
#USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR=""

# 1 creates $USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR/usr/bin and symlinks it as ~/usr, so that software you build
# with --prefix=$HOME/usr persists across instances. Requires USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR.
# Default: 1
#USER_ENV_ENABLE_USR_SYMLINK=1

# Comma-separated local installer scripts to copy onto the shared file system
# and run once per boot. Requires USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR. For each entry:
#
#   * The file name must be install_<name> with no extension, because <NAME>_OPT
#     is derived from it by uppercasing; a dot would produce an invalid variable
#     name.
#   * You must also define <NAME>_OPT in this config, even if empty - it is
#     expanded without a default.
#   * The script is copied to $USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR/opt/bin/install_<name>, made executable,
#     and run as EC2_SSH_USERNAME with three arguments:
#         "$USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR/opt"  "$USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR/usr"  $<NAME>_OPT
#     so it should install into argument 2 and build in argument 1.
#
# Example:
#   USER_ENV_INSTALLER_SCRIPTS="./my_work/install_mosh,./my_work/install_neovim"
#   MOSH_OPT="1.4.0"
#   NEOVIM_OPT="v0.10.2"
#USER_ENV_INSTALLER_SCRIPTS=""

# Dotfiles kept on the shared file system. Requires USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR. Entries
# are created under $USER_ENV_ROOT_DIR/dotfiles/ and linked into the home
# directory. Use USER_ENV_DOTFILES_FILE for individual files, such as
# .bash_history; USER_ENV_DOTFILES_DIR for directories that should be replaced
# by a symlink, such as .cache or .local; and USER_ENV_DOTFILES_DIR_CONTENTS for
# directories whose files, subdirectories, and dotfiles should be linked
# individually while keeping the directory itself in place, such as .vim.
# Existing non-symlink files are replaced. Do not use both directory settings
# for the same directory.
# Example:
#    USER_ENV_DOTFILES_FILE=".bash_history"
#    USER_ENV_DOTFILES_DIR=".cache,.local"
#    USER_ENV_DOTFILES_DIR_CONTENTS=".vim"
#USER_ENV_DOTFILES_FILE=""
#USER_ENV_DOTFILES_DIR=""
#USER_ENV_DOTFILES_DIR_CONTENTS=""

# Same, but relative to ~/.config/ instead of the home directory.
#USER_ENV_CONFIG_DOTFILES_FILE=""
#USER_ENV_CONFIG_DOTFILES_DIR=""
#USER_ENV_CONFIG_DOTFILES_DIR_CONTENTS=""

# Local files copied to the instance during first-boot setup. Each array entry
# is one mapping in the form source|destination|permission. The destination
# and permission may be empty; an empty destination keeps the source path and
# an empty permission preserves the local file mode.
# `~` in a source means the local home directory; in a destination it means the
# remote EC2_SSH_USERNAME home directory. Parent directories are created.
# The `|` character separates fields and cannot be used in a path.
# The file contents are embedded in EC2 user-data (Base64 is encoding, not
# encryption). User-data can be read from inside the instance and by AWS
# principals with suitable permissions, so treat this as an explicit setting
# for copying credentials and private keys.
# Example:
#   INSTANCE_COPY_ENTRIES=(
#     "${HOME}/.ssh/known_hosts|~/.ssh/known_hosts|"
#     "${HOME}/.ssh/config|~/.ssh/config|600"
#     "~/.localrc||644"
#   )
#INSTANCE_COPY_ENTRIES=()

# Local shell script appended verbatim to the end of the generated user-data, so
# it runs as root after everything above. This is the hook for site-specific
# setup: cloning repositories, installing language runtimes, registering
# monitoring agents. Review it before every launch - it ends up inside the
# instance metadata. A missing file is skipped silently.
#INSTANCE_USER_DATA_EXTRA_SCRIPT=""
